}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is \n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}a & -5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & b & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & c\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-36400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall  that the standard basis of ${R}^{3}$ consists of the vectors ${e}_{1},{e}_{2},$ and ${e}_{3},$ given by\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $B$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n[{b}_{1}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{2}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[{b}_{3}]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -5 & 0 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=-3,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-36400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/line