 change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $S.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2} & 1 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & \\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113415" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 7 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-4)-7\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ -7 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ 4 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 3 \\\\ -7 & -4\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-35799" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -7 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-9)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} & -\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 3 & \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $B$ to the basis $C$ is given by \n${P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}].$ \nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 7-3\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -5 & 2\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-81856" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $C$ to the basis $B$ is given by\n${P}_{C\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$  ." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $C$ to $B.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of ${P}_{C\\to B}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C} & ={P}_{C\\to B}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7\\cdot 4-3\\cdot 9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -9 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 9 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 4\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}9 & -7 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113413" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 \\\\ -9 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -9 & 6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}6 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113520" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-36398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}]}$ and $C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}$ be bases of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-36398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -3 & -8 & 3 & -9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & -2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 7 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}7 & -5 \\\\ -3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-36398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db50