}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & -2 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113513" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113513" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $B={[\\begin{aligned}17 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$  be a basis of ${R}^{2}$ and let $S$ be the standard basis of ${R}^{2}.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $S$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{e}_{1},{e}_{2}}$, respectively. Recall that the standard basis of ${R}^{2}$ consists of the vectors\n\n$$\n{e}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{e}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $S$ relative to the basis $B.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${e}_{1}$ and ${e}_{2}$ relative to the basis $B.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & {e}_{1} & {e}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}17 & 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{17}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{17} & -\\frac{4}{17} & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=17{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & \\frac{4}{17} & \\frac{1}{17} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-\\frac{4}{17}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 17\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $S$ to $B.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{S\\to B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 17\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/state.json" "file_sha256" "1bcf1521b865fb1acf6ed0120325be8fb604644d04ed78732d71304d5847c209"} {"question" "q-113515" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9033a7a857858aa2623b7114ae34d4374fd3f0a185272c6b9db507039da7f4f2"} {"question" "q-113515" "field" "selection" "attribut