eld" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/e62970e0d84f224be492efe709d4df42872cbcc9e68c136e2f73b0e36e18f5c4.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/5f5ad323ae8875717d552f068c827295954ecdb2c802f5fb3899c18884aa1f1f.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4832.json" "file_sha256" "b66b8b3ba844578f597b4b68b105c8bbf55971fe3bc84420a559f4470b3d96fd"} {"question" "e-4833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$ Find the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ if \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/827f1a79a51ea88e59f5d766ee531cacf3945cc2ce649521c7b1d8e7eea35e06.png)\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/82d0eab6a51dbf1615a3e2bb4fc6ffef622a38ac3c980dc742b552946c11a87a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4833.json" "file_sha256" "f683341febb2dd556537116a6eae2e061648de2b1106dc1e405f9413f8eefabb"} {"question" "e-4833" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Recall  that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2,$ and $\\mathbf e_3,$ given by\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/0d9a23830f48d4659dd963110d9322c12e8d4849bd9bcd63ae9d5adb0e1fba05.png)\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/10b5f2e35da56c7be6d547827164c68f475df4d012116b9666c12a94bbc39f55.png)\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}.$ \n\nTherefore, we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/06ef110e61fcdf044e15300d50bc5ac918b37e8351c1aa6b77124dd2029c9aa3.png)\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=0,$ and $c=10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4833.json" "file_sha256" "f683341febb2dd556537116a6eae2e061648de2b1106dc1e405f9413f8eefabb"} {"question" "e-4835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/07a8e394234737d51a0064816b255569d20e826eb3a82884a24f1fc0a9fa6baa.png)\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4835.json" "file_sha256" "eeaac6a87137fe1253a3f17d70375e0bed58b41e203b5504d8630eb446659f05"} {"question" "e-4835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{C\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 5-(-6)\\cdot 3}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4835.json" "file_sha256" "eeaac6a87137fe1253a3f17d70375e0bed58b41e203b5504d8630eb446659f05"} {"question" "e-4837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/51b47f81b941fffa62d1f8402a4ef08b9b7d1636b1a37a86578311f1db192b3d.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/a9964c0672c8859f4b4857d422e25a435ed0be907c074a22b7cc12729f051613.png) be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$ Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4837.json" "file_sha256" "3585b072088b481ef81837dfb5fc039fbf3af4a2b781e905623e01399d8a9486"} {"question" "e-4837" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{ \\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{ \\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\},$ respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/assets/60eb4bbd057feab1b78fb283e951dc8c35f9a95875bd46f078c87c25b3b749c8.png)\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}= & [\\begin{aligned}\\,4 & 22 \\\\ \\,1 & 13\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/example-4837.json" "file_sha256" "3585b072088b481ef81837dfb5fc039fbf3af4a2b781e905623e01399d8a9486"} {"question" "q-81394" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$a=2,$ $b=2,$ and $c=5$"] "category" "reviewed_ma_solution" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14065461/answer-source-reviews.json" "file_sha256" "905e14bea259995730a477f41e34ff4147a26af147acae3fcb362d2e6b3b33ca" "reviewer_session" "01a118cc-89a0-7c90-9221-d6599ae13bca" "rationale" "Derived judgment: the change-of-coordinates matrix has columns [b1]_C=[1,2,-1], [b2]_C=[0,2