{c}_1=\\mathbf{b}_2-4\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{c}_2=5\\mathbf{b}_1+7\\mathbf{b}_2$, find the change-of-coordinates matrix $P_{\\small\\mathcal{C}\\to\\mathcal{B}}$ from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$."] [17592186124219 :question/worked-solution "The coordinates of the vectors in $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ are\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{C}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{B}$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124220 :question/problem "Let $\\mathcal{B}=\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2,\\mathbf{b}_3\\}$ be a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ and let $\\mathcal{S}$ be the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$. Find the values of $a,b$, and $c$ if\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & a & 2 \\\\ -3 & 7 & b \\\\ c & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124220 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the standard basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consists of the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$, and $\\mathbf e_3$, given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe components of the vectors in $\\mathcal{B}$ are just the coordinates relative to the standard basis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 10\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{S}$ is a matrix whose respective columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{S}$.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ -3 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 10 & -4 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nFinally, $a=0$, $b=0$, and $c=10$."] [17592186124221 :question/problem "The change-of-coordinates matrix from the basis $\\mathcal{B}$ to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$ is given by\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$\nFind the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$."] [17592186124221 :question/worked-solution "The change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{C}$ to $\\mathcal{B}$ is the inverse of the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$. Therefore, we simply need to find the inverse of the given matrix.\n\nComputing the inverse of $P_{\\small\\mathcal{B}\\to\\mathcal{C}}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_{C → B} &= P_{B → C}^{-1} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -6 \\\\ 3 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^{-1} \\\\\n&= (1)/((-4) \\cdot 5 - (-6) \\cdot 3)\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 6 \\\\ -3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{5}{2} & -3 \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186124222 :question/problem "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ be bases of $\\mathbb{R}^2$. Find the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$."] [17592186124222 :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ and $\\mathcal{C}$ as $\\{\\mathbf{b}_1,\\mathbf{b}_2 \\}$ and $\\{\\mathbf{c}_1,\\mathbf{c}_2 \\}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors of $\\mathcal{B}$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$.\n\nSo, we need to find the coordinates of $\\mathbf{b}_1$ and $\\mathbf{b}_2$ relative to the basis $\\mathcal{C}$. We can do this simultaneously by considering the matrix\n$\\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid; c_{1}\\begin{vmatrix}c_{2} & b_{1}\\end{vmatrix}b_{2}; \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid \\mid$\n\nNow, we simply reduce the left-hand side to reduced row-echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2} &:=R_{2} + (-3)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix} & R_{1}\\end{vmatrix}:=R_{1} + 2R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 4 & 22 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the right-hand side, we obtain the change-of-coordinates matrix from $\\mathcal{B}$ to $\\mathcal{C}$. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nP_{B → C} = \\mid \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 22 \\\\ 1 & 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122009 17592186122010 17592186122011 17592186122012 17592186122013 17592186122369 17592186122370 17592186122371 17592186122372 17592186122373 17592186122405 17592186122406 17592186122407 17592186122408 17592186122409 17592186123887 17592186123888 17592186123889 17592186123890 17592186123891] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122008 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122008 :answer-field/id] [17592186122008 :answer-field/key] [17592186122008 :answer-field/type] [17592186122368 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122368 :answer-field/id] [17592186122368 :answer-field/key] [17592186122368 :answer-field/type] [17592186122404 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122404 :answer-field/id] [17592186122404 :answer-field/key] [17592186122404 :answer-field/type] [17592186123886 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123886 :answer-field/id] [17592186123886 :answer-field/key] [17592186123886 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
